== Configuration ==

=== YAML configuration file ===

The main configuration is a YAML file. Example (<code>config_instance1.yaml</code>):

<pre>
server_name: "Backend-1"
database_path: "/opt/charger/data/central_system_1.db"
web_port: 9001
ocpp_port: 9000
</pre>

{| class="wikitable"
! Key !! Default !! Description
|-
| <code>server_name</code> || <code>Backend</code> || Display name shown in the web interface header
|-
| <code>database_path</code> || <code>/opt/charger/data/csms.db</code> || Path to the SQLite database file
|-
| <code>web_port</code> || <code>9001</code> || Port for the Flask web interface (HTTP)
|-
| <code>ocpp_port</code> || <code>9000</code> || Port for the OCPP WebSocket server
|}

{{Note|If the OCPP port has been changed from the web interface, the value stored in the database takes precedence over the YAML/default value at startup.}}

=== Running multiple instances ===

Multiple CSMS instances can run on the same host by giving each its own configuration file with distinct <code>database_path</code>, <code>web_port</code>, and <code>ocpp_port</code> values (for example <code>config_instance1.yaml</code> and <code>config_instance2.yaml</code>). Run each with its own <code>--config</code> argument or its own systemd unit.

=== OCPP configuration (web UI) ===

Open '''OCPP Config''' in the web interface to adjust protocol settings without editing files:

* '''Heartbeat interval''' — expected keep‑alive period from chargers.
* '''Connection expiration''' — when a charger is considered offline.
* '''OCPP port''' — the WebSocket listening port; this can be changed at runtime and is persisted in the database.

=== Email notification configuration ===

Configure email alerts on the '''Mail Config''' page. Settings are stored in the database and a '''test email''' can be sent to verify delivery.

{| class="wikitable"
! Setting !! Description
|-
| <code>enabled</code> || Enable/disable email notifications
|-
| <code>protocol</code> || <code>smtp</code>, <code>starttls</code>, or <code>smtps</code>
|-
| <code>host</code> / <code>port</code> || SMTP server address and port
|-
| <code>from</code> || Sender email address
|-
| <code>username</code> / <code>password</code> || SMTP authentication credentials (password masked in the UI)
|-
| <code>recipients</code> || One or more recipient addresses
|-
| <code>notify_boot</code> || Notify on charger boot
|-
| <code>notify_faulted</code> || Notify when a connector faults
|-
| <code>notify_connect</code> / <code>notify_disconnect</code> || Notify on charger connect/disconnect
|-
| <code>notify_start_transaction</code> / <code>notify_end_transaction</code> || Notify on session start/stop
|-
| <code>notify_ev_suspended</code> || Notify when the EV stops drawing power (e.g. battery full)
|}

=== Zonneplan configuration ===

Zonneplan integration provides hourly electricity tariffs for smart charging and cost calculation. When no tariff data is available, a default of '''€0.30/kWh''' is used (configurable in app settings).

Set it up from the '''Zonneplan Config''' page:

# Enter your Zonneplan account '''email''' and start the login. Zonneplan emails a login link.
# Click the link in your inbox, then '''verify''' in the CSMS to complete the OAuth2 authentication.
# '''Set up the connection''' by selecting your electricity connection.

Once connected, the CSMS periodically fetches hourly tariffs (stored in the database) and uses them for cost breakdowns and smart‑charging recommendations. Tokens are refreshed automatically.

